Problem: Detect and Remove Loop in Linked List
Link: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/remove-loop-in-linked-list/1

Problem Description:

Given the head of a singly linked list, the task is to remove a cycle if present. A cycle exists when a node's next pointer points back to a previous node, forming a loop. Internally, a variable pos denotes the index of the node where the cycle starts, but it is not passed as a parameter. The terminal will print true if a cycle is removed otherwise, it will print false.

Examples:

Input: head = 1 -> 3 -> 4, pos = 2
Output: true
Explanation: The linked list looks like

A loop is present in the list, and it is removed.
Input: head = 1 -> 8 -> 3 -> 4, pos = 0
Output: true
Explanation: 

The Linked list does not contains any loop. 
Input: head = 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4, pos = 1
Output: true
Explanation: The linked list looks like 

A loop is present in the list, and it is removed.
Constraints:
1 ≤ size of linked list ≤ 105

CODE:

/*
class Node {
  public:
    int data;
    Node* next;

    Node(int val) {
        data = val;
        next = nullptr;
    }
};
*/
class Solution {
  public:
    void removeLoop(Node* head) {
        // code here
        //Return the node which is beginning of the loop.

        //1st: Brute-force Approach:
        //Using vector to store the addresses of the node
        // & at each address, 
        // check if it is already present return that particular addr node.

        //2nd: Perform the same using HashMap/HashSet which helps you achieve the same
        //in 0(1) T.C.

        //3rd Using slow-fast pointers

        //Find the (POI) point of intersection
        //initiate slow with head && fast will remain there itself (POI).
        //increment both by one step at a time,
        //as and when they meet, it is the beginning of the loop.

        //4th Approach:
        //Similar to above, just few minor changes,
        //Once we find (POI)
        //Calculate the length of the loop,
        //Initiate slow & fast = head,
        //Increment fast until it reaches fast = slow + length of the loop
        //Now increment both, 
        //as and when slow reaches the node which is the beginning of the loop
        //Mathematically both should point to the same node.
        //Reason: Because distance between slow & fast = length of loop.

        if(!head || !head->next)
        return;

        Node* slow = head;
        Node* fast = head;

        while(fast && fast->next) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;

            if(slow==fast)
            break;
        }

        if(!fast || !fast->next)
        return;

        //We already got POI,
        //initiating slow = head;
        slow = head;
        while(slow!=fast) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next;
        }
        
        //IMP: Reason why we didn't used prev pointer in the above iteration itself,
        //If the beginning of the loop = start of the LL, 
        //we might run into memory-bound exception, prev = NULL, prev->next == NULL (x->error)
        //Example case: 1->2->3->4 pos = 1 (4 connecting back to 1), DRY-RUN IT.

        //We got the beginning of the loop,
        //traverse one more time until slow->next == fast & disconnect from that point.
        slow = slow->next;
        while(slow->next!=fast) {
            slow = slow->next;
        }
        
        //slow->next == fast, disconnecting it.
        slow->next = NULL;
    }
};
